{Abstract}

As well known, the spectrum of a non-relativistic two-body system interacting by the Coulomb potential is the Balmer series
E_{n}=\frac{\alpha^{2}m}{4n^{2}}

produced by the Schrödinger equation. In 1954, Wick and Cutkosky have found, in the Bethe-Salpeter equation framework, that for
\alpha>\frac{\pi}{4}

the relativistic effects result in new levels (in addition to the Balmer series). However, the physical nature of these new states remained unclear and therefore their existence was being questioned.
